WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:05.000
Remember the café with numbered tickets?

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.945
You order, you get a number, and the coffees come out in whatever order they're ready.

3
00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:12.009
HTTP/2 works the same way.

4
00:00:12.009 --> 00:00:17.573
Many requests share one connection, and each one gets a number, called a stream.

5
00:00:17.833 --> 00:00:19.223
So here's a question.

6
00:00:19.223 --> 00:00:24.649
If one packet on that connection gets lost, how many requests have to wait for it?

7
00:00:24.649 --> 00:00:25.972
Most people say one.

8
00:00:25.972 --> 00:00:27.097
Keep your answer.

9
00:00:27.333 --> 00:00:28.479
Let's shrink it.

10
00:00:28.479 --> 00:00:33.634
Your app asks the bank for three answers at once, on streams 1, 3 and 5.

11
00:00:33.634 --> 00:00:36.569
The bank needs 300 milliseconds for each.

12
00:00:36.833 --> 00:00:40.809
Then it sends each answer, 1,000 bytes, in one segment.

13
00:00:40.809 --> 00:00:44.207
A segment is TCP's word for one of its packets.

14
00:00:44.467 --> 00:00:46.952
The path takes 50 milliseconds each way.

15
00:00:46.952 --> 00:00:52.544
With nothing lost, all three answers arrive together, about half a second after the start.

16
00:00:52.767 --> 00:00:57.108
Now we drop one segment on purpose, the one carrying stream 1.

17
00:00:57.108 --> 00:00:59.209
Segments 2 and 3 arrive whole.

18
00:00:59.209 --> 00:01:00.889
Which requests are late?

19
00:01:00.889 --> 00:01:02.009
Pause and guess.

20
00:01:05.233 --> 00:01:06.014
All three.

21
00:01:06.014 --> 00:01:09.684
Every one of them, about 120 milliseconds late.

22
00:01:09.967 --> 00:01:10.844
Here's why.

23
00:01:10.844 --> 00:01:15.072
TCP promises one stream of bytes, delivered in order.

24
00:01:15.072 --> 00:01:23.128
To TCP, these three answers are bytes 1 to 3,000 of that one stream, and the first 1,000 are missing.

25
00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:29.240
So segments 2 and 3 wait in TCP's receive buffer, complete, and your app gets nothing.

26
00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:34.741
Stream 5 has its whole answer, sitting on the counter, and nobody may pick it up.

27
00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:38.352
Your app's computer keeps asking for the missing bytes.

28
00:01:38.352 --> 00:01:41.521
About a round trip later, the bank sends them again.

29
00:01:41.521 --> 00:01:44.812
The hole fills, and TCP hands everything over at once.

30
00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:46.553
Here's the café version.

31
00:01:46.553 --> 00:01:52.931
The kitchen makes coffees in any order, but every tray leaves through one narrow hatch, in single file.

32
00:01:53.900 --> 00:02:00.627
Drop one tray, and every tray behind it waits until that coffee is made again, even the ones that are ready.

33
00:02:00.867 --> 00:02:03.139
The hatch is the TCP connection.

34
00:02:03.139 --> 00:02:04.772
The trays are segments.

35
00:02:04.772 --> 00:02:06.689
And the orders are streams.

36
00:02:06.933 --> 00:02:13.671
This has a name: head-of-line blocking, the first in line holding up everyone behind.

37
00:02:13.671 --> 00:02:18.744
HTTP/2 removed it from HTTP, and kept it one layer down, in TCP.

38
00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.493
So, go back to HTTP/1.1, with three connections, three hatches?

39
00:02:23.493 --> 00:02:25.776
The lesson's lab tried that too.

40
00:02:25.776 --> 00:02:30.412
The loss hits only one request, and the other two finish on time.

41
00:02:30.633 --> 00:02:33.746
But the unlucky one was 330 milliseconds late.

42
00:02:33.746 --> 00:02:39.768
With no segment behind its hole, nothing told the bank quickly, so it waited for a timer.

43
00:02:39.768 --> 00:02:43.219
And every extra connection pays its own handshakes.

44
00:02:43.467 --> 00:02:47.803
The real fix gives every order its own hatch, on one connection.

45
00:02:47.803 --> 00:02:50.513
That's QUIC, the transport under HTTP/3.

46
00:02:50.513 --> 00:02:54.985
It runs over UDP, and numbers the bytes of each stream separately.

47
00:02:55.233 --> 00:02:56.733
Lose the same segment.

48
00:02:56.733 --> 00:03:01.572
Segments 2 and 3 complete streams 3 and 5, and go straight to your app.

49
00:03:01.572 --> 00:03:03.957
Only stream 1 waits for its resend.

50
00:03:04.200 --> 00:03:06.934
Should everything leave HTTP/2, then?

51
00:03:06.934 --> 00:03:07.820
Not so fast.

52
00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:16.242
Inside a data center, a building full of servers, loss is rare and a round trip takes a fraction of a millisecond.

53
00:03:16.242 --> 00:03:19.715
There, one HTTP/2 connection is a fine default.

54
00:03:19.967 --> 00:03:27.860
On a phone with 1 percent loss, a busy connection hits several holes a second, and each one stalls every request in flight.

55
00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:32.801
One loss also slows the whole connection's sending, for every stream at once.

56
00:03:33.033 --> 00:03:34.516
So measure under loss.

57
00:03:34.516 --> 00:03:39.233
Testing on a clean office network is like testing an umbrella indoors.

58
00:03:39.733 --> 00:03:43.846
So, how many requests wait for one lost packet?

59
00:03:43.846 --> 00:03:47.084
On HTTP/2, every one behind the hole.

60
00:03:47.084 --> 00:03:51.721
HTTP/2 removed the wait in HTTP, not the wait in TCP.

61
00:03:52.267 --> 00:03:53.548
One quick check.

62
00:03:53.548 --> 00:04:00.274
This time segment 3 is lost, the one carrying stream 5, and segments 1 and 2 arrive.

63
00:04:00.274 --> 00:04:02.916
Over HTTP/2, which requests wait?

64
00:04:02.916 --> 00:04:04.197
Pause and think.

65
00:04:07.433 --> 00:04:08.481
Only stream 5.

66
00:04:08.481 --> 00:04:13.719
Segments 1 and 2 came before the hole, so TCP hands them over on time.

67
00:04:13.719 --> 00:04:16.563
A hole only holds up what's behind it.
